            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Making Decisions in Code</h2>
                    <p>Programs need to make decisions based on conditions. Should we show an error message? Is the user logged in? Is the score high enough to pass? In this lesson, you'll learn how to control the flow of your program using conditional statements.</p>
                    
                    <p>By the end of this lesson, you'll be able to write programs that respond differently based on various conditions.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>The if Statement</h2>
                    <p>The <code>if</code> statement executes code only when a condition is true:</p>

                    <div class="syntax-box">
                        <code>if (condition) { // code to execute }</code>
                    </div>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">int age = 20;

if (age &gt;= 18)
{
    Console.WriteLine("You are an adult.");
}

// The condition must be a boolean expression
bool isLoggedIn = true;

if (isLoggedIn)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Welcome back!");
}</code></pre>

                    <div class="flow-diagram">
                        <div class="flow-box start">Start</div>
                        <div class="flow-arrow">↓</div>
                        <div class="flow-box condition">age &gt;= 18?</div>
                        <div class="flow-branches">
                            <div class="flow-branch">
                                <span class="branch-label yes">Yes</span>
                                <div class="flow-arrow">↓</div>
                                <div class="flow-box action">Print "You are an adult"</div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="flow-branch">
                                <span class="branch-label no">No</span>
                                <div class="flow-arrow">↓</div>
                                <div class="flow-box skip">(skip)</div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-arrow">↓</div>
                        <div class="flow-box end">Continue</div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>The if-else Statement</h2>
                    <p>Use <code>else</code> to specify code that runs when the condition is false:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">int temperature = 25;

if (temperature &gt; 30)
{
    Console.WriteLine("It's hot outside!");
}
else
{
    Console.WriteLine("The weather is pleasant.");
}

// Another example
int score = 45;

if (score &gt;= 50)
{
    Console.WriteLine("You passed!");
}
else
{
    Console.WriteLine("You failed. Try again!");
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>The else-if Chain</h2>
                    <p>Use <code>else if</code> to check multiple conditions in sequence:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">int score = 85;
string grade;

if (score &gt;= 90)
{
    grade = "A";
}
else if (score &gt;= 80)
{
    grade = "B";
}
else if (score &gt;= 70)
{
    grade = "C";
}
else if (score &gt;= 60)
{
    grade = "D";
}
else
{
    grade = "F";
}

Console.WriteLine($"Your grade is: {grade}");</code></pre>

                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Important</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>Conditions are evaluated from top to bottom. Once a condition is true, its code runs and the rest are skipped. Order matters!</p>
                    </div>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Order matters! Put more specific conditions first
int age = 15;

// ❌ Wrong order - the first condition catches everything
if (age &gt;= 0)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Valid age");
}
else if (age &gt;= 18)  // This never runs!
{
    Console.WriteLine("Adult");
}

// ✅ Correct order
if (age &gt;= 18)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Adult");
}
else if (age &gt;= 0)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Valid age, but not an adult");
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Nested if Statements</h2>
                    <p>You can put if statements inside other if statements:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">bool hasTicket = true;
int age = 15;

if (hasTicket)
{
    if (age &gt;= 18)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Welcome to the adult section!");
    }
    else
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Welcome to the general section!");
    }
}
else
{
    Console.WriteLine("Please buy a ticket first.");
}</code></pre>

                    <div class="info-box success">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Tip: Simplify with Logical Operators</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>You can often replace nested ifs with logical operators for cleaner code.</p>
                    </div>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Instead of nested ifs:
if (hasTicket)
{
    if (age &gt;= 18)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Adult with ticket");
    }
}

// Use logical AND:
if (hasTicket &amp;&amp; age &gt;= 18)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Adult with ticket");
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>The switch Statement</h2>
                    <p>When you have many conditions checking the same variable, use <code>switch</code>:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">int dayNumber = 3;
string dayName;

switch (dayNumber)
{
    case 1:
        dayName = "Monday";
        break;
    case 2:
        dayName = "Tuesday";
        break;
    case 3:
        dayName = "Wednesday";
        break;
    case 4:
        dayName = "Thursday";
        break;
    case 5:
        dayName = "Friday";
        break;
    case 6:
        dayName = "Saturday";
        break;
    case 7:
        dayName = "Sunday";
        break;
    default:
        dayName = "Invalid day";
        break;
}

Console.WriteLine(dayName);  // Wednesday</code></pre>

                    <h3>Key Points About switch</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>case</code> labels specify values to match</li>
                        <li><code>break</code> exits the switch (required in C#)</li>
                        <li><code>default</code> handles unmatched values (optional but recommended)</li>
                        <li>Works with integers, strings, chars, and enums</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h3>Multiple Cases</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">char grade = 'B';

switch (grade)
{
    case 'A':
    case 'B':
        Console.WriteLine("Great job!");
        break;
    case 'C':
        Console.WriteLine("Good effort!");
        break;
    case 'D':
    case 'F':
        Console.WriteLine("Need improvement.");
        break;
    default:
        Console.WriteLine("Invalid grade.");
        break;
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Switch Expressions (Modern C#)</h2>
                    <p>C# 8+ introduced a more concise <strong>switch expression</strong> syntax:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">int dayNumber = 3;

// Switch expression - returns a value
string dayName = dayNumber switch
{
    1 =&gt; "Monday",
    2 =&gt; "Tuesday",
    3 =&gt; "Wednesday",
    4 =&gt; "Thursday",
    5 =&gt; "Friday",
    6 =&gt; "Saturday",
    7 =&gt; "Sunday",
    _ =&gt; "Invalid day"  // _ is the discard pattern (like default)
};

Console.WriteLine(dayName);  // Wednesday</code></pre>

                    <h3>Switch Expressions with Patterns</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">int score = 85;

string grade = score switch
{
    &gt;= 90 =&gt; "A",
    &gt;= 80 =&gt; "B",
    &gt;= 70 =&gt; "C",
    &gt;= 60 =&gt; "D",
    _ =&gt; "F"
};

// With multiple conditions
string category = score switch
{
    &gt;= 90 and &lt;= 100 =&gt; "Excellent",
    &gt;= 70 and &lt; 90 =&gt; "Good",
    &gt;= 50 and &lt; 70 =&gt; "Average",
    &gt;= 0 and &lt; 50 =&gt; "Needs Improvement",
    _ =&gt; "Invalid score"
};</code></pre>

                    <div class="comparison-box">
                        <div class="comparison-item">
                            <h4>Switch Statement</h4>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Executes code blocks</li>
                                <li>Requires <code>break</code></li>
                                <li>Multiple statements per case</li>
                                <li>Can have side effects</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                        <div class="comparison-item">
                            <h4>Switch Expression</h4>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Returns a value</li>
                                <li>No <code>break</code> needed</li>
                                <li>Single expression per arm</li>
                                <li>More concise</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Pattern Matching</h2>
                    <p>Modern C# supports powerful pattern matching in conditionals:</p>

                    <h3>Type Patterns</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">object value = "Hello";

if (value is string text)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"It's a string: {text.ToUpper()}");
}
else if (value is int number)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"It's a number: {number * 2}");
}

// In a switch expression
object data = 42;

string result = data switch
{
    string s =&gt; $"String of length {s.Length}",
    int n =&gt; $"Integer: {n}",
    null =&gt; "Null value",
    _ =&gt; "Unknown type"
};</code></pre>

                    <h3>Property Patterns</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Check object properties
var person = new { Name = "Alice", Age = 25 };

string message = person switch
{
    { Age: &lt; 18 } =&gt; "Minor",
    { Age: &gt;= 18, Age: &lt; 65 } =&gt; "Adult",
    { Age: &gt;= 65 } =&gt; "Senior",
    _ =&gt; "Unknown"
};</code></pre>

                    <h3>Logical Patterns</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">int temperature = 25;

string weather = temperature switch
{
    &lt; 0 =&gt; "Freezing",
    &gt;= 0 and &lt; 15 =&gt; "Cold",
    &gt;= 15 and &lt; 25 =&gt; "Mild",
    &gt;= 25 and &lt; 35 =&gt; "Warm",
    &gt;= 35 =&gt; "Hot"
};

// Using 'not' pattern
if (value is not null)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Value exists!");
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>

                    <div class="mistake-box">
                        <h4>❌ Using = Instead of ==</h4>
                        <pre><code class="language-csharp">int x = 5;

// Wrong - this is assignment, not comparison!
if (x = 10)  // Compile error in C#

// Correct
if (x == 10)</code></pre>
                    </div>

                    <div class="mistake-box">
                        <h4>❌ Forgetting Braces</h4>
                        <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Dangerous - only the first line is in the if
if (isValid)
    Console.WriteLine("Valid");
    DoSomething();  // This ALWAYS runs!

// Safe - use braces
if (isValid)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Valid");
    DoSomething();
}</code></pre>
                    </div>

                    <div class="mistake-box">
                        <h4>❌ Comparing Floating-Point Numbers</h4>
                        <pre><code class="language-csharp">double a = 0.1 + 0.2;
double b = 0.3;

// Risky - floating-point precision issues
if (a == b)  // Might be false!

// Better - check if they're "close enough"
if (Math.Abs(a - b) &lt; 0.0001)</code></pre>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <LessonTakeaways>
                    <LessonTakeaway><code>if</code> statements execute code when a condition is true.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway><code>else</code> provides an alternative when the condition is false.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway><code>else if</code> chains allow checking multiple conditions.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway><code>switch</code> is cleaner when comparing one value to many options.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Switch expressions and pattern matching make code more concise.</LessonTakeaway>
                </LessonTakeaways>

